Can anyone help me with this?<div><br></div><div>I really don&#39;t understand why the red line and black line for the baseline did not overlap (the data are the same).</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks<br><br>On Tuesday, March 5, 2013 2:56:45 PM UTC-5, Qingyang Li wrote:<blockquote class="gmail_quote" style="margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex">







<p></p><h1 style="margin-bottom:18px;color:rgb(64,64,64);line-height:36px;font-size:30px;font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif">A scaling problem of ggplot2&#39;s geom_violin -- Scaling is different for two geom_violin layers </h1><p style="margin-bottom:9px;color:rgb(115,115,115);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif">Hi all,</p><p style="margin-bottom:9px;color:rgb(115,115,115);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif">I have some data which I need to plot their distribution.<br>The data I have are three measures from a subject, and the meausers were taken under three conditions (baseline, cond1, cond2).<br>What I wanted to do is to plot the distribution of cond 1 and cond2 with baseline as background. I followed <a href="https://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups=#!topic/ggplot2-dev/u8jDvD_jSRg" style="color:rgb(0,105,214)" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups%3D%23!topic/ggplot2-dev/u8jDvD_jSRg&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792372973041000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3QUwX54WUDD-xXs1FPtI0U">Brandon Hurr&#39;s suggestion</a> and used 2 layers with alpha. As a quality check, I also plotted baseline on both the layers. What I found is that the baseline in the two layers are not the same.<br>Since my data files were too large, I am going to demostrate the problem using random numbers. In the figure generated, the red lines are my data, and the black lines are background. As you can see the red lines and black lines for baseline are not always overlap.</p><h2 style="color:rgb(64,64,64);line-height:36px;font-size:24px;font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif">Here is the code.</h2><p style="margin-bottom:9px;color:rgb(115,115,115);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif"><em>please keep in mind that the output of the code varies since the data are randomly generated</em></p><pre style="font-family:Monaco,&#39;Andale Mono&#39;,&#39;Courier New&#39;,monospace;padding:14px;margin-bottom:18px;line-height:16px;font-size:11px;border:1px solid rgb(217,217,217);white-space:pre-wrap;word-wrap:break-word;color:rgb(115,115,115)"><code style="font-family:Monaco,&#39;Andale Mono&#39;,&#39;Courier New&#39;,monospace;border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px">library(ggplot2)

#predifine variables
value = c();Cond = c();measure = c();
value1 = c(); #as background

measures = c(&quot;measure1&quot;,&quot;measure2&quot;,&quot;measure3&quot;);
Conditions = c(&quot;baseline&quot;,&quot;cond1&quot;,&quot;cond2&quot;);
# prepare data.
n=1000

for (i in seq(1:length(measures))) {
  Valuetemp1=&#39;&#39;; 

  for (j in seq(1:length(Conditions))) {

    Valuetemp=c(runif(n,0,1))
    if (j==1){Valuetemp1=Valuetemp} #valuetemp1 store the data for baseline

    value = c(value,Valuetemp);
    value1 = c(value1,Valuetemp1); # this is background
    Cond = c(Cond,rep(Conditions[j], length(Valuetemp)));
    measure = c(measure,rep(measures[i], length(Valuetemp)));
    }
  }

Cond=factor(Cond);

#generte data Frame for ploting
df = data.frame(
  index = 1:length(value), 
  measure,
  Cond,
  value
)
df1 = data.frame(
  index = 1:length(value), 
  measure,
  Cond,
  value1)

# ploting
p = ggplot()

p + 
  geom_violin(data=df1, mapping=aes(Cond, value1)) +
  geom_violin(data=df, mapping=aes(Cond, value),alpha=0.5, color = &quot;red&quot;) + 
                facet_grid(measure ~ . , scales = &quot;fixed&quot;, space = &quot;fixed&quot;) +
                ggtitle(&quot;Distribution&quot;) +
                xlab(&quot;Conditions&quot;) +
                ylab(&quot;Value&quot;)
</code></pre><p style="margin-bottom:9px;color:rgb(115,115,115);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif">Here is the result figure I got.</p><p style="margin-bottom:9px;color:rgb(115,115,115);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif"><img src="https://ci6.googleusercontent.com/proxy/jLbmXwUHI7AAt7yYte4fm4qfWHBKmSXi7FuvnWM3zVfxltt2k6wMyePUXKZFUQ-r_mwt1FE=s0-d-e1-ft#http://i48.tinypic.com/10gi369.png" alt="plot of chunk unnamed-chunk-1"></p><p style="margin-bottom:9px;color:rgb(115,115,115);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif">Does anyone know why this happen and how can I fix it?</p><p style="margin-bottom:9px;color:rgb(115,115,115);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif">Thanks</p><p style="margin-bottom:9px;color:rgb(115,115,115);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,&#39;Hiragino Sans GB&#39;,Arial,sans-serif">Qingyang Li</p><p></p></blockquote></div>
